00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:56.910
On Mount Breakin Ridge, above the northeast corner of Harrison Lake, Adam Kelliger is lifting quartz and citrine off ground a glacier still covered a year ago, the Vancouver Sun Reports.

00:03:56.910 --> 00:04:03.490
He is 36, from Vanderhoof, and he owns Rubble Rock and Gems at 250 Southwest Marine Drive

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:06.150
among the country's largest gem in fossil shops.

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:10.830
He says that ICE has pulled back at least 3 meters in the past year, opening pockets

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:13.370
the weather and the freeze-thaw cycle have not scarred.

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:18.410
He studied environmental science at Lingara, plus some philosophy, and he says he became

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:20.170
an artist because neither of those paid.

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:24.450
15 years on, the shop has replaced what he calls the starving artist years.

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:26.250
He still cares about the climate.

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:31.370
he told the son. And in the same breath, he is cheering the glacier on. He says he has not sold

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:36.070
his soul for the money alone. Courts from the King Satrine mine at Harrison is threaded with

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:40.470
green needles of actinolite. The yellow Satrine often wears an amethyst on top,

00:04:40.850 --> 00:04:45.530
a scepter he says shows up nowhere else, in holes you could sit in, packed with thousands of

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:50.470
finger-sized crystals. Chloe Monty, a master student in glaciology at Simon Fraser,

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:56.010
led a study that found the Whistler, Horseman and Blackcomb glaciers have lost between 60 and 95

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:59.590
percent of their area, and are projected to be gone by the turn of the century.

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:05.310
Whistler likely lasts about a decade. Horseman 10 to 50 years. Blackcomb 20 to 70.

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:10.550
She said it was shocking set against photos from the 1970s and that the ice is shrinking almost

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:15.650
in real time when that change should be nearly invisible on a human scale. Brian Menunos at the

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:20.390
University of Northern British Columbia tracked a faster loss in this province and in Switzerland

00:05:20.390 --> 00:05:23.610
with a lidar steady, a climate loop that feeds itself.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:26.170
Here, wild firesuit darkens the ice,

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:28.350
warms the surface, and speeds the melt.

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:32.290
Switzerland has lost nearly 20% of its ice volume in five years,

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:34.410
with Saharan dust doing the same job.

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:37.270
Brent Ward, in Earth Sciences at Simon Fraser,

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:39.930
said early spring heat waves now strip the snow,

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:42.370
so later summer heat hits ice directly,

00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:43.970
and FireSuit makes it worse.

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:46.010
He called it a cascading hazard.

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:49.650
Only a few alpine glaciers remain from the cordiler and ice sheet

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:54.790
that covered this region 17,000 years ago and reached 120 kilometers south of Seattle.

00:05:55.470 --> 00:06:00.410
Ward said the ice on Kelleher's claim looked unhealthy, bare of snow, losing mass fast.

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:05.310
In Switzerland, the retreat has uncovered bunkers, munitions, and bombs from the First World

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:05.670
War.

